13:30:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Plastics are made of long-chain molecules with tightly bound electrons, meaning they have a large energy gap between their occupied and unoccupied electron states. Visible light photons don't have enough energy to excite electrons across this gap, so the light passes through without being absorbed. In contrast, metals have a "sea" of free electrons that can easily absorb and re-emit photons across the visible spectrum, making them shiny and opaque. Even very thin metal films reflect or absorb most light, so they appear reflective or dark rather than transparent. While you can create a semi-transparent metal by making it extremely thin (like a few nanometers), it still isn't truly transparent like glass or plastic. The fundamental electronic structure of metals prevents bulk transparency in the visible range.
